WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.157
The frameworks: if this capability, then these safeguards.

2
00:00:03.437 --> 00:00:16.472
All three large Western labs publish a framework with the same skeleton: define dangerous capability thresholds, evaluate each model against them, and commit to safeguards before deployment past a threshold.

3
00:00:16.752 --> 00:00:20.357
METR maintains an index of these policies with their versions.

4
00:00:20.637 --> 00:00:23.112
Anthropic’s Responsible Scaling Policy.

5
00:00:23.392 --> 00:00:27.189
First published in September 2023; version 3.

6
00:00:27.469 --> 00:00:35.512
0 (24 February 2026) was a comprehensive rewrite, and the current version at the time of review is 3.

7
00:00:35.792 --> 00:00:38.885
4, effective 8 July 2026.

8
00:00:39.165 --> 00:00:48.723
It ties required safeguards to capability thresholds in areas such as chemical and biological weapons and AI research and development (3.

9
00:00:49.003 --> 00:00:55.424
4 split the AI R&D threshold into two levels and added a CBRN development threshold).

10
00:00:55.704 --> 00:01:13.837
Version 3 added public Risk Reports, which must indicate where material was redacted, and a Frontier Safety Roadmap of safety goals, and later point releases let the Long-Term Benefit Trust (an independent body in Anthropic’s governance) request external review of Risk Reports.

11
00:01:14.117 --> 00:01:16.187
OpenAI’s Preparedness Framework.

12
00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:22.739
Version 2 (15 April 2025) remains the governing text at the time of review.

13
00:01:23.019 --> 00:01:29.483
It names three Tracked Categories: biological and chemical, cybersecurity, and AI self-improvement.

14
00:01:29.763 --> 00:01:40.216
A model that reaches a High threshold is not deployed until its risks are sufficiently minimized; one that reaches Critical needs safeguards even during development.

15
00:01:40.496 --> 00:01:48.197
A Safety Advisory Group of OpenAI leaders recommends safeguards, leadership decides, and a board committee oversees.

16
00:01:48.477 --> 00:01:53.747
It also defines Research Categories (risks that need more work before they can be measured).

17
00:01:54.027 --> 00:02:02.581
In May 2026 OpenAI added a Frontier Governance Framework that maps its practices onto new legal requirements.

18
00:02:02.861 --> 00:02:05.357
Google DeepMind’s Frontier Safety Framework.

19
00:02:05.637 --> 00:02:06.597
Version 3.

20
00:02:06.877 --> 00:02:27.016
1 (17 April 2026) defines Critical Capability Levels for CBRN, cyber, and harmful manipulation, plus machine learning R&D and misalignment, and introduces lower Tracked Capability Levels so that significant but not severe risks are caught earlier and handled proportionately.

21
00:02:27.296 --> 00:02:37.707
The shared weakness is also structural: the company writes the thresholds, runs most evaluations, decides whether a threshold was crossed, and can revise the policy.

22
00:02:37.987 --> 00:02:52.280
That is why the frontier of this debate in 2026 is external verification: Anthropic’s LTBT review, and Amodei’s September 2026 proposal for embedded third-party evaluators.
